  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nOur primary approach employs a convex optimization framework to minimize the maximum overlap integral for Erd\u0151s' problem. We utilize the initial construction as a starting point and iteratively refine it using gradient-based updates and feasibility repair, ensuring adherence to the constraints at each step.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nWe represent h as a vector in \u211d^n where n = n_points. Each h[i] \u2208 [0, 1], and sum(h) = n/2. The objective is to minimize C\u2085 = max_{k} (convolution(h, 1\u2212h, k) * dx). We model the overlaps as a linear function of h.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\na. Validate and Normalize Input\n   - Initialize h with initial_h_values if provided, else start with uniform distribution scaled to sum(h) = n/2.\n   - Ensure sum(h) = n/2 and h[i] \u2208 [0,1].\n\nb. Core Optimization Loop\n   i. Compute the current overlap vector via convolution.\n   ii. Identify the maximum overlap and its corresponding lag.\n   iii. Decrease h at positions contributing to this maximum overlap.\n   iv. Clip h to [0, 1] and adjust sum to maintain constraint.\n   v. Repeat until convergence or time budget exhausted.\n\nc. Feasibility Repair\n   - After each update, clip h to [0, 1].\n   - Enforce sum(h) = n/2 using a scaling factor.\n\nd. Termination\n   - Stop early if the improvement falls below a threshold.\n   - Always return the best solution found so far.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\nWe use a gradient descent approach with momentum. Parameters include step size (0.01), momentum (0.9), and improvement threshold (1e-4). Variations include different initializations (uniform, random, etc.) and alternative clipping strategies.\n\n5. Compute Budget Management\nThe algorithm runs within the specified time budget, stopping expensive loops early if necessary. The dominant cost is the convolution computation, which is O(n\u00b2). For n = 1000, this is feasible within 1100s on a single CPU.\n\n6. Validation Steps\nBefore returning, ensure h satisfies sum(h) = n/2 and h[i] \u2208 [0,1]. Recompute C\u2085 directly using the evaluator to verify consistency.",
